TI  - Genetic Improvement of the Fatty Acid Biosynthesis System to Alter the ω-6/ω-3 Ratio in the Soybean Seed
T2  - JOURNAL OF THE AMERICAN OIL CHEMISTS SOCIETY
AB  - A high omega-6/omega-3 fatty acid ratio in the soybean seed adversely affects human health. The objective of the present study was to improve the fatty acid biosynthesis to reduce the omega-6/omega-3 ratio by combining the FAD2-1A and FAD2-1B mutant alleles with alpha-linolenic acid (omega-3) related alleles from wild soybean. The F-2 population comprising 2320 F (2:3) lines developed from S08-14717 x PI 483463 cross exhibited significant variation for fatty acid components. Of these, 114 lines were advanced to the F (5:6) generation and genotyped for FAD2-1A and FAD2-1B alleles. The lines carrying mutant FAD2-1A and FAD2-1B alleles showed similar to 761 g kg(-1) oleic, and similar to 50 g kg(-1) linoleic acids, which reduced omega-6/omega-3 ratios to similar to 0.6. Conversely, the lines carrying FAD2-1A or FAD2-1B mutant alleles had 267 or 399 g kg(-1) oleic, 327 or 471 g kg(-1) linoleic, and 120 or 130 g kg(-1) alpha-linolenic acids concentration, respectively. The elevated alpha-linolenic acid resulted in the reduction of omega-6/omega-3 ratios in the range 2.5-3.9. The present study demonstrated that combining FAD2 mutant alleles with alpha-linolenic acid-related alleles from wild soybean reduces the seed omega-6/omega-3 ratio.
