TI  - Alpha linolenic acid intake alleviates myocardial ischemia/reperfusion injury via the P2X7R/NF-κB signalling pathway
T2  - JOURNAL OF FUNCTIONAL FOODS
AB  - This study investigated the protective role of a-linolenic acid (ALA) in myocardial ischemia/reperfusion (MI/R) injury. ALA was indicated to dose-dependently protect myocardium against I/R injury, attenuate purinergic P2X7 receptor (P2X7R) activation, prevent NF-kappa B13 activation, and inhibit tumor necrosis factor (TNF)-alpha and interleukin-1 beta (IL-1 beta) production in I/R myocardium. Overexpression of P2X7R enhanced NF-kappa B activation, inflammation and MI/R injury, which could be alleviated by ALA. In vitro hypoxia/reoxygenation (H/R) H9c2 cells, ALA could also inhibit P2X7R activation, NF-kappa 3 activation and TNF-alpha and IL-1 beta production. Molecular docking analysis indicated ALA may hamper the activation of P2X7R. The functional relevance of ALA directly regulated P2X7R and the subsequent NF-kappa B signalling was supported by inhibition of P2X7R with A438079 (P2X7R antagonist) as well as suppression of NF-kappa B by PDTC (NF-KB inhibitor) in H9c2 H/R cells transfected with pcDNA3.0-P2X7R plasmid. These findings demonstrated that ALA attenuates inflammation and MI/R injury through down-regulating P2X7R/NF-kappa B signalling pathway.
