TI  - Association between Plasma Omega-3 and Handgrip Strength According to Glycohemoglobin Levels in Older Adults: Results from NHANES 2011-2012
T2  - NUTRIENTS
AB  - Background: Low muscle strength is a predictor of mortality in older adults. Although the evidence concerning hyperglycemia is limited, evidence shows that omega-3 (omega-3) intake may be positively associated with muscle strength. However, the association between plasma omega-3 and muscle strength in older adults according to glycohemoglobin (HbA1c) levels has not yet been investigated. Objective: To evaluate whether plasma omega-3 levels are associated with handgrip strength in individuals over 50 years according to HbA1c levels. Methods: This cross-sectional study included 950 older adults (50-85 years) from NHANES 2011-2012. Linear regression analysis was performed to evaluate the association between plasma omega-3 and handgrip strength in individuals with elevated (>= 5.7%) or normal HbA1c levels after adjustments for confounders. Results: Total plasma omega-3, docosahexaenoic acid, eicosapentaenoic acid and alpha-linolenic acid were not associated with handgrip strength in older adults regardless of HbA1c levels. Conclusion: Plasma omega-3 levels are not associated with handgrip strength in individuals over 50 years old independent of HbA1c levels.
