TI  - Erythrocyte ω-3 polyunsaturated fatty acids are inversely associated with the risk of oral cancer: a case-control study
T2  - NUTRITION & DIABETES
AB  - Objectives Evidence about omega-3 polyunsaturated fatty acids (omega-3 PUFAs) and oral cancer risk were limited. We aimed to evaluate the association of erythrocyte omega-3 PUFAs with the risk of oral cancer in a population from China. Methods Erythrocyte omega-3 PUFAs of 236 oral cancer patients and 300 controls were determined by gas chromatography. Restricted cubic spline and logistic regression were used to analyze the association between erythrocyte omega-3 PUFAs and oral cancer risk. The crude and adjusted OR with 95% CI was calculated. Stratification analysis was performed to explore the potential interaction between omega-3 PUFAs and other traditional risk factors such as smoking and drinking. Results Eicosapentaenoic acids (EPA), docosahexaenoic acids (DHA) and omega-3 index were negatively but non-linearly related to risk of oral cancer as observed by restricted cubic spline. The adjustedORof EPA, DHA, and omega-3 index were 0.52 (95%CI: 0.35-0.76), 0.19 (95%CI: 0.08-0.44), 0.20 (95%CI:0.09-0.44), respectively. Stratification analysis showed that the adverse correlation between EPA and oral cancer was only significant in the non-smoking group, while the adverse correlation of alpha-linolenic acid (ALA), EPA, and DHA were only significant in the non-drinking group. General multiplicative interactions were observed between omega-3 PUFAs and smoking or drinking. Conclusions Adverse but non-linear associations were observed between erythrocyte EPA, DHA, omega-3 index, and oral cancer risk. Additionally, there were multiplicative interactions between omega-3 PUFAs and other behavior factors such as smoking and drinking. The protective effect of omega-3 PUFAs maybe more significant in the non-smoking or non-drinking population.
